{"seq":null,"source_sha256":"d45730ae8512188e03d768ea37c4e43f8854f82ab91b2b1610a027ac4aee5734","commit_to":{"owner":"teamwork-inc-test","repo":"e2e-team-repo","branch":"main"},"doc_stem":"2026-10-13-2-e2e-default-ref-1791886379208","status":"completed","machine":{"spec_version":10,"spec_hash":"88f0116f3ef6"},"checkpoint":"navigate","run_id":"mp-eng-c6a7d7cc","payload":{"expand_iris":[],"focus":"Default ref test: omitted ref creates a topic branch; testing reference/branch creation behavior for a document submitted by Tanya TeamOwner","rationale":"The document is a one-line pipeline test (\"Testing that omitted ref creates a topic branch\") with no product, people, or decision content beyond the submitter. I called perspective once with a focus on ref/branch behavior and the submitter. The map's only rich area is Narrative_AntiSlackPositioning (product positioning), and the focus-scoped tail surfaced sprint-4 positioning and webhook-architecture positions, style observations, and the sprint retro. None of these bear on refs, branches, or this test, so expanding them would be reaching. The Thematic door lists zero areas and no dimensions or unplaced items relate to branching, so I deliberately expanded nothing.","n8n_execution_id":"160159","expansion_count":0,"context":"# Perspective — teamwork-inc-test/e2e-team-repo @ 84b969c0d479fe6f964b477df2d59c34f3907b74\npin: 8cd7b8157d94d9e7930f8d9928f69f4c78c7dc9c\n\nSections: (1) THE MAP — this graph's richest ground, full narration; (2) COVERAGE — what's active, contested, unplaced, and quiet across everything else, meaning inside whisper-brackets; (3) DOORS — complete axes (areas, people, dimensions, documents, unplaced), each one expand away; (4) FOCUS — material loaded for your focus, LAST. The map, coverage, and doors never vary with your focus.\n\n^-marked entries are compressed stubs carrying child addresses. Their narration and any surfaced witness quotes are verbatim-accurate — quote them as-is — but a stub carries no provenance (source, date, supersession). Expand (aswritten_expand with the IRIs you need) before any claim that turns on WHEN or by WHOM something was said, or whether it is still the current framing. Witness-depth sections below already carry full provenance.\n\nThis entry is BOUNDED — the whole graph is deliberately not inlined. Every node is reachable within a bounded number of expands: before concluding the perspective is silent on your subject, check the map and coverage, then expand the door whose axis would hold it (^Thematic for topics and areas, ^People for anyone, ^Documents for a call or memory, ^Unplaced for loose positions). Absence becomes signal only after the relevant door shows nothing.\n\n# The map — this graph's richest ground (top fidelity band)\n\n## ^Narrative_AntiSlackPositioning  [4 children · 20260310]\nTanya frames the product against Slack's real-time model. The anti-Slack label is the primary positioning thesis: every conversation should produce a persistent decision artifact.\n  decisions-under 1 · evidence 4 — expand ^Narrative_AntiSlackPositioning for the full child index\n\n# Coverage — narrated, from four directions\n\nActive: [ Position_ContextPreservationNextSprint · Next sprint focus: context preservation — linking decisions to t… ^Position_ContextPreservationNextSprint ] · [ Position_AntiPatternRealtimeChat · Real-time chat (Slack, Teams) is the named anti-pattern. The pro… ^Position_AntiPatternRealtimeChat ] — the newest witnessed work, one document each.\n\n# Doors — pick an axis\n\n[ ^Thematic — every area (0) by name ] [ ^People 0 ] [ ^Dimensions — 0 axes ] [ ^Documents 2 ] [ ^Unplaced 0 ]\nEach door expands to a complete axis: aswritten_expand(iris=[\"Thematic\"]) — same coords as this perspective. Nothing in the graph is outside every door.\n\n# Focus: Default ref test: omitted ref creates a topic branch; testing reference/branch creation behavior for a document submitted by Tanya TeamOwner (witness depth — per-call section)\n\n### Position_Sprint4ThesisProven\ntype: Position\nwitnessed: 20260322\nsource: Sample_Sprint4Retro_20260322\nTanya: 'This sprint proved the thesis.' The widget intercepts the moment of consensus in any chat thread and creates a persistent decision record with full context, requiring zero extra steps.\nunder: PositioningThesis, Narrative_ZeroEffortCapture\n\n### StyleObs_Sprint4_DirectQuote\ntype: StyleObservation\nwitnessed: 20260322\nsource: Sample_Sprint4Retro_20260322\ntrue\nDocument uses direct first-person quotes from named executives (Tanya, Tina) to anchor every key decision. Quotes are attributed inline with speaker name and colon.\n> \"This sprint proved the thesis. The widget intercepts the moment someone says 'let's go with option B'\"\nunder: ToneDirectPersonal\n\n### Position_ProductCategoryAsyncPlatform\ntype: Position\nwitnessed: 20260310\nsource: Sample_TeamworkProductVision_20260310\nProduct category explicitly defined as 'Structured async decision platform' — a deliberate category creation move against real-time chat tools.\nunder: Narrative_AntiSlackPositioning\n\n### StyleObs_ToneDirectPersonal_Quotes\ntype: StyleObservation\nwitnessed: 20260315\nsource: Sample_CustomerDiscovery_DecisionFatigue_20260315\ntrue\nDocument voice is built almost entirely from direct first-person quotes from interviewees, lending immediacy and credibility. Interviewer synthesis is minimal and framing-only.\n> \"We tried Notion for decision logs. Nobody updates it. The decision happens in Slack and the Notion page stays empty.\"\nunder: ToneDirectPersonal\n\n### Narrative_AntiSlackPositioning\ntype: Narrative\nwitnessed: 20260310\nsource: Sample_TeamworkProductVision_20260310\nTanya frames the product against Slack's real-time model. The anti-Slack label is the primary positioning thesis: every conversation should produce a persistent decision artifact.\nTeamwork Inc positions itself as the anti-Slack: structured async decisions over real-time noise\nunder: PositioningThesis\n\n### Obs_TanyaAntiSlackQuote\ntype: Observation\nwitnessed: 20260310\nsource: Sample_TeamworkProductVision_20260310\nTanya's verbatim framing of the anti-Slack positioning thesis.\nunder: PositioningThesis\n\n### Position_WebhookArchitecture\ntype: Position\nwitnessed: 20260322\nsource: Sample_Sprint4Retro_20260322\nArchitecture decision: event-driven capture via webhook, not polling. Tanya: 'Polling would kill us at scale. Webhooks mean we only process actual decisions, not every message.'\nunder: StackOverview, Narrative_ZeroEffortCapture\n\n### Sample_Sprint4Retro_20260322\ntype: Sample\nwitnessed: 20260322\nSprint retrospective for the async decision widget feature. Participants: Tanya Owner (CEO), Tina Teammate (Head of Product), Dev Team. Covers shipped feature, key decisions, and blockers resolved.\nSprint 4 Retro: Async Decision Widget\n","positions":"","pin":"8cd7b8157d94d9e7930f8d9928f69f4c78c7dc9c","positions_consult":"{\"positions\":[]}","context_bytes":5492},"live_run_id":"2041a30ccfbe","at":"2026-10-13T10:13:09Z"}
